MULTIMETER USE

1  AC VOLTAGE TEST
   Test for AC Voltage
   - Set Dial to V~ (ACV)
   - Example: 230V AC
   Probes in wall outlet live/neutral

2  DC VOLTAGE TEST
   Test for DC Voltage
   - Set Dial to V= (DCV)
   - Example: 12.6V DC
   Probes on battery + and -

3  CONTINUITY TEST
   Check for Continuity
   A sound (BEEP) means a completed circuit
   - Set Dial to the Sound Icon
   Probes across a resistor or wire

4  PHASE & NEUTRAL IDENTIFY
   Identify Phase and Neutral Lines
   - Probe to check Phase (Live) wire
   - Caution: High Voltage
   Outlet: L (Phase)  N (Neutral)

5  RESISTANCE TEST
   Measure Resistance (ohm)
   - Set Dial to appropriate ohm range
   - Example: 1000 ohm
   Probes across resistor (power OFF)

6  DIODE TEST
   Test Diode Health
   - Check for Forward Voltage (0.5V - 0.8V)
   - Reverse probe for check
   Forward: 0.5V-0.8V   Reverse: OL / open

7  CAPACITOR TEST
   Test Capacitor Function
   - Set Dial to F (Capacitance)
   - Charge / Discharge check
   Example cap: 47uF 25V

8  FUSE TEST
   Check Fuse Condition
   Beep = OK
   No Beep = Blown
   Use Continuity Mode
   Probes on both ends of fuse

9  DC CURRENT TEST
   Measure DC Current (A)
   - Set Dial to mA / A (DCA)
   - Insert Meter in Series
   - Example: 0.85A
   Circuit: battery -> meter -> load

IMPORTANT TIPS
1. Select the Correct Range before probing
2. Ensure Probe Leads are in the correct sockets (COM, V/ohm/mA, 10A)
3. Disconnect power for Resistance / Continuity tests
4. Use Extra Caution with High Voltage (AC)
5. Turn Off the Meter after use to save battery

SYMBOLS GUIDE
V~     AC Voltage
V=     DC Voltage
A=     DC Current
ohm    Resistance
speaker Continuity
diode  Diode
||-    Capacitor

FUNDAMENTALS
- Voltage (V): potential difference. AC alternates, DC is constant polarity
- Current (A): flow of charge. Always measure in SERIES. Never put current mode across a voltage source
- Resistance (ohm): opposition to current. Power must be OFF. R = V / I
- Continuity: very low resistance path. Beep if closed circuit
- Diode: conducts one way. Forward drop typically 0.5-0.8V for silicon
- Capacitor: stores charge. Measure capacitance (F, uF, nF) after discharge
- Fuse: sacrificial low-resistance link. Continuity = good
- Probe sockets: black always COM. Red in V/ohm/mA for most tests, 10A jack only for high current
- Range: start high if unsure, then drop range for better resolution
- Safety: CAT rating on meter, never exceed voltage/current limits, one hand rule on live AC when possible
